From ud640f02d29e7de63 at groups.google.com  Wed Jun  8 22:53:19 2016
From: ud640f02d29e7de63 at groups.google.com (Mark B)
Date: Wed, 08 Jun 2016 22:53:19 +0000
Subject: How to interpret abundance coefficients from gmultmix?
Message-ID: <LqNdm-z-hyk.o68o0blIFAAJ@unmarked.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/unmarked/c/LqNdm-z-hyk/m/o68o0blIFAAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hello,

In some cases, the summary of an unmarked model indicates the scale on which the regression coefficients (estimates) are provided. For example, see page 9 of the dynamic occupancy models vignette.

This doesn't seem to be the case for gmultmix. Here's part of my model summary:

Call:
gmultmix(lambdaformula = ~LAREA_0000 + REBU_0300 + REGS_0300,
    phiformula = ~1, pformula = ~1, data = umfGMM, mixture = "NB")

Abundance:
            Estimate     SE     z  P(>|z|)
(Intercept)    1.821 0.0305 59.77 0.00e+00
LAREA_0000     1.240 0.0245 50.66 0.00e+00
REBU_0300     -0.116 0.0231 -5.03 4.91e-07
REGS_0300     -0.103 0.0276 -3.74 1.84e-04

[truncated]

The scale of the regression coefficients (i.e., estimates) on abundance isn't indicated, so I don't know how to interpret (or transform) them.

I'd like to transform the above regression coefficients to their natural scale so I can interpret them in the usual way (i.e., a one unit change in X results in a beta change in abundance). Following an example in the unmarked overview,I tried this:

lc <- linearComb(AreaBurnHarv_nb, c(1,0,0,0), type="lambda") #intercept =1, all covariates = 0 (their mean)
backTransform(lc)

Backtransformed linear combination(s) of Abundance estimate(s)

 Estimate    SE LinComb (Intercept) LAREA_0000 REBU_0300 REGS_0300
     6.18 0.188    1.82           1          0         0         0

Transformation: exp

...however this isn't resulting in coefficients on their natural scale, but (I think) abundance estimates when all covariates are set to the mean values. In other words, there are 6.18 critters when the covariates are at their mean.

I am obviously missing something, and would appreciate any help with transforming the betas to their natural scale.

Thanks,

Mark B.
Ph.D. Candidate
University of Saskatchewan

From u527d9b3ec7deff2d at groups.google.com  Thu Jun  9 15:30:09 2016
From: u527d9b3ec7deff2d at groups.google.com (Dan Linden)
Date: Thu, 09 Jun 2016 15:30:09 +0000
Subject: Re: How to interpret abundance coefficients from gmultmix?
Message-ID: <LqNdm-z-hyk.iYR-Xh9_FAAJ@unmarked.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <LqNdm-z-hyk.o68o0blIFAAJ@unmarked.groups.google.com>
References: <LqNdm-z-hyk.o68o0blIFAAJ@unmarked.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/unmarked/c/LqNdm-z-hyk/m/iYR-Xh9_FAAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi Mark,

I'd suggest getting yourself a copy of the AHM <http://www.mbr-pwrc.usgs.gov/pubanalysis/keryroylebook/> book that Marc and Andy wrote.  This kind of material is covered there (and many other places), and it has less to do with unmarked than it does generalized linear modeling.  Your regression coefficients are not meaningful on a natural scale, aside from how they affect predictions of log-scale abundance which can themselves be back-transformed.  The gmultmix output for abundance is no different than any other Poisson or Negative Binomial regression.

From u0f206837070c34f7 at groups.google.com  Thu Jun  9 18:29:04 2016
From: u0f206837070c34f7 at groups.google.com (Sarah Thompson)
Date: Thu, 09 Jun 2016 18:29:04 +0000
Subject: Time-removal for detection with an occupancy model..
Message-ID: <hUKndzA-KIM.s_SWD0KLBAAJ@unmarked.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/unmarked/c/hUKndzA-KIM/m/s_SWD0KLBAAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi unmarked users --

Does anyone know if any occupancy models in unmarked allow time-removal methods for assessing detection, or could be altered to accommodate that? Possibly by treating 10 equal time intervals as repeated visits in some fashion (maybe by altering data to presume that once a species is detected, it is detected in all subsequent time intervals).

I also have repeated samples, but I would like to compare results of the 2 methods, with the goal of trying to isolate availability from presence (somewhat).

Conversely, would it be inappropriate to use multinomPois() on presence/absence data, if I don't use parameters in the abundance side of the model & only look at detection? I think that is the only model that specifically accommodates removal sampling.

Any advice or relevant papers on the topic would be appreciated!

Thanks,

Sarah Thompson

From u00ebeb6e0521112d at groups.google.com  Thu Jun  9 18:33:32 2016
From: u00ebeb6e0521112d at groups.google.com (Kery Marc)
Date: Thu, 09 Jun 2016 18:33:32 +0000
Subject: RE: [unmarked] Time-removal for detection with an occupancy model..
Message-ID: <hUKndzA-KIM.6Xwrf4CLBAAJ@unmarked.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <hUKndzA-KIM.s_SWD0KLBAAJ@unmarked.groups.google.com>
References: <hUKndzA-KIM.s_SWD0KLBAAJ@unmarked.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/unmarked/c/hUKndzA-KIM/m/6Xwrf4CLBAAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Dear Sarah,

I think you can use any traditional occupancy modeling software to fit occupancy models with a removal design by simply setting all observations after first detection to missing. At least, that's what we wrote in the AHM1 book (see page 621), after observing that this yielded exactly the same answers as the more classical formulation of the model using a multinomial distribution for the detection history frequencies.

Best regards  --- Marc

