Dear Alvaro,

I think you need to be a little more specific about what you want to do. From what I understand, I assume that QGIS is some GIS software with which you want to produce pretty maps of your species distribution.

Species distribution can naturally be represented as the occurrence probability = occupancy probability. So what you want to do (I presume) is obtain the predicted occupancy probability for each site and year and then stuff that into the GIS program in whichever way that software accepts data input (perhaps as a text file).

You are unsure about which model is best to use for prediction of occupancy, so you fit models that differ in their covariates. Now, you can (1) either take the low-AIC model and base inferences on that single model or (2) you can average over all models, weighing each by its AIC.

Once you have decided how you want to treat model uncertainty, you have the parameter estimates from a single model or the model-averaged estimates from an ensemble of the models, for the following parameters:
- psi1: occupancy probability in year 1
- ext: extinction probability in each interval from one year to the next
- col: same for colonisation
- and p: detection probability

To obtain predictions for occupancy for year 2 onwards, you can first use a predict function for psi1 to obtain occupancy predictions in year one. Then, you need to get predictions for ext and col for every interval after the first year, because you need those to obtain the predictions of occupancy in years 2 until the final year in a recursive manner as follows:

psi_t+1 = psi_t * (1-eps_1) + (1 - psi_t) * col_t
(This makes sense: the occupied sites now are the sum of those that were occupied last year and didn't go extinct and those that were not occupied last year and became colonized.)

So, using this recursion, and site-specific estimates of psi1, ext and col, you can obtain the predicted occupancy at each site for years 2 until the final year. If you want to get standard errors around these predictions for year 2 until the last year, you would have to devise a bootstrap. Alternatively, you can fit the model in BUGS, and then computation of these derived quantities, along with the uncertainty (SEs), becomes much more easy.

This will be covered in a chapter in AHM2 and if you like we could send you some very rough draft section on how to obtain site-specific occupancy predictions for all years in a dynamic occupancy analysis.

Best regards  --- Marc

